0756072998046875, 0.0106201171875, -0.0482177734375, -0.01523590087890625, -0.0262298583984375, 0.006237030029296875, -0.03668212890625, -0.00672149658203125, 0.0278778076171875, 0.002532958984375, -0.007083892822265625, 0.0017795562744140625, 0.01255035400390625, 0.0229339599609375, 0.0162811279296875, -0.041656494140625, -0.007678985595703125, -0.008636474609375, -0.00354766845703125, 0.0077056884765625, 0.00037217140197753906, 0.007068634033203125, -0.0178985595703125, 0.00757598876953125, 0.005695343017578125, -0.009765625, 0.0032825469970703125, -0.01678466796875, -0.04425048828125, 0.0216064453125, 0.0144195556640625, 0.0016384124755859375, 0.0085906982421875, 0.01158905029296875, -0.002063751220703125, 0.01436614990234375, -0.00605010986328125, -0.01126861572265625, -0.021942138671875, 0.0217132568359375, 0.001262664794921875, -0.023834228515625, 0.007724761962890625, 0.01348876953125, -0.019256591796875, 0.0211944580078125, -0.006488800048828125, 0.00704193115234375, 0.0081024169921875, -0.01084136962890625, 0.0257720947265625, -0.0142364501953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimera states\u2014is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. Here, \"spatiotemporal patterns\" refer to the structured arrangements of oscillator phases across space and time, for example, the propagation of phase waves in neural tissue or the coexistence of synchronized and desynchronized regions in coupled oscillator arrays. However, identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains challenging. Existing methods often focus on global synchronization (e.g., Kuramoto order parameter) or amplitude-based measures (such as envelope correlation or amplitude variance), which can obscure spatially localized phase relationships. For instance, global order parameters cannot distinguish between different spatial organizations that yield the same overall synchrony, and amplitude-based metrics may fail to detect phase waves or clusters when amplitude remains constant (see e.g., Breakspear et al., 2010; Buzs\u00e1ki & Draguhn, 2004, \"Neuronal oscillations in cortical networks\"; and Schreiber, 2000, \"Measuring information transfer\"). These limitations motivate the need for methods that directly capture spatial phase organization.\n\nTo address this gap, we ask: How can we robustly and efficiently characterize the evolving spatial organization of phases in oscillatory systems, including during nonstationary or partially synchronized regimes? We focus on phase rather than amplitude because phase relationships more directly capture synchronization and wave propagation phenomena that are central to many applications, such as neural dynamics. Empirically, phase-based analyses have been shown to reveal traveling waves and phase clusters even when amplitude remains unchanged (Breakspear et al., 2010; Rubino et al., 2006, \"Propagating waves mediate information transfer in the motor cortex\"). Moreover, amplitude fluctuations can arise from noise or unrelated processes, potentially confounding the detection of genuine phase organization. Combined measures may dilute the sensitivity to phase-specific features, as demonstrated in comparative studies (see Supplementary Table 1 for quantitative benchmarks).\n\n**Comparison to Prior Symbolic/Ordinal Methods and Unique Contributions**\n\nSymbolic and ordinal pattern analysis methods have previously been used to characterize temporal dynamics in time series, such as permutation entropy (Bandt & Pompe, 2002) and symbolic transfer entropy (Staniek & Lehnertz, 2008). Some studies have extended ordinal analysis to spatial domains (e.g., Zunino et al., 2012, \"Permutation-information-theory approach to unveil delay dynamics from time-series analysis\"), but these approaches typically focus on temporal ordering or global spatial statistics, and often do not explicitly encode local spatial phase relationships or handle phase wrapping. Our framework differs by (1) directly encoding the local spatial ordering of oscillator phases at each time point, (2) introducing a tie symbol to robustly capture phase plateaus or clusters, and (3) normalizing entropy to enable comparison across system sizes and symbol sets. Unlike prior symbolic approaches, our method is specifically tailored to oscillatory systems with circular phase variables, and is designed to distinguish subtle differences in spatial phase organization, even when global synchrony or amplitude-based measures are similar.\n\n**Algorithmic Details and Reproducibility**\n\nHere, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. Specifically, for each oscillator and its spatial neighbors, we construct a symbolic pattern by ranking the phases. To clarify, the algorithm proceeds as follows:\n\n1. For each oscillator \\(i\\), identify its spatial neighborhood (e.g., for a linear array, the set \\(\\{i-1, i, i+1\\}\\)). For networks with boundaries, neighborhoods are defined using periodic boundary conditions (i.e., indices wrap around the array), unless otherwise specified.\n2. Extract the phases \\((\\phi_{i-1}, \\phi_i, \\phi_{i+1})\\) at the current time point. Phases are defined on \\([0, 2\\pi)\\), and phase differences are computed modulo \\(2\\pi\\) to account for circularity.\n3. Sort these phases in ascending order. Assign a symbol corresponding to the resulting permutation (e.g., \"012\" for \\(\\phi_{i-1} < \\phi_i < \\phi_{i+1}\\), \"021\" for \\(\\phi_{i-1} < \\phi_{i+1} < \\phi_i\\), etc.).\n4. If any pair of neighboring phases are nearly equal (i.e., \\(|\\phi_{\\pi(j)} - \\phi_{\\pi(j+1)}| < \\epsilon\\) for any \\(j\\), where \\(\\epsilon\\) is a small threshold, typically set as a fraction of \\(2\\pi\\); in our experiments, \\(\\epsilon = 0.05 \\times 2\\pi\\)), assign a special \"tie\" symbol to indicate a plateau or cluster. The choice of \\(\\epsilon\\) is discussed below.\n5. Repeat for all oscillators and time points.\n\nFor example, if the phases in a neighborhood are (0.1, 0.5, 0.2), the sorted order is (0.1, 0.2, 0.5), corresponding to the permutation \"102\". If \\(\\phi_i\\) and \\(\\phi_{i+1}\\) differ by less than \\(\\epsilon\\), a tie symbol is assigned. This symbolic encoding thus captures both gradients and plateaus in phase. The full algorithmic details, including handling of boundary conditions and tie-breaking, are provided in Supplementary Algorithm 1, which also includes pseudocode and computational complexity analysis. In brief, for ties involving more than two phases, all tied phases are grouped and assigned a unique symbol; ties are resolved consistently across the network.\n\n**Definition of Key Terms**\n\n- *Spatial ordinal pattern*: The permutation (or tie symbol) representing the relative ordering of phases within a local spatial neighborhood at a given time.\n- *Spatial permutation entropy*: The Shannon entropy of the distribution of spatial ordinal patterns across the network at a given time point, normalized by the maximum possible entropy for the symbol set.\n\n**Parameter Sensitivity and Justification**\n\nThe choice of neighborhood size determines the spatial scale of pattern detection. For nearest-neighbor coupling (as in our synthetic benchmarks), a neighborhood size of three (the oscillator and its two immediate neighbors) captures local gradients and clusters. Larger neighborhoods can detect broader spatial structures but may dilute sensitivity to fine-scale patterns. In our EEG application, neighborhoods are defined by a distance threshold (3 cm) to accommodate irregular electrode layouts, balancing spatial resolution and robustness to noise.\n\nThe tie threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) controls the granularity of cluster detection. We set \\(\\epsilon = 0.05 \\times 2\\pi\\) (i.e., 5% of the phase circle), based on preliminary sensitivity analysis: increasing \\(\\epsilon\\) above this value led to over-detection of ties and reduced discrimination between regimes, while smaller values made the method sensitive to noise-induced phase fluctuations. Supplementary Figure 2 shows that classification accuracy and entropy values are stable for \\(\\epsilon\\) in the range 0.03\u20130.07 (normalized units), and for neighborhood sizes between 3 and 5. We recommend selecting \\(\\epsilon\\) based on the expected phase noise and the desired sensitivity to clustering.\n\n**Spatial Permutation Entropy Calculation**\n\nThis symbolic representation encodes local spatial ordering relations, capturing both phase gradients and synchronized clusters within a single framework. From these patterns, we define a spatial permutation entropy \\(H(t)\\) at each time point \\(t\\) as\n\n\\[\nH(t) = -\\sum_s p_s(t) \\log p_s(t),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p_s(t)\\) is the probability of observing symbol \\(s\\) across all neighborhoods in the network at time \\(t\\). To facilitate interpretation, we normalize \\(H(t)\\) by dividing by the maximum possible entropy \\(H_{\\text{max}} = \\log(N_s)\\), where \\(N_s\\) is the total number of possible symbols (including tie symbols) for the chosen neighborhood size. Thus, normalized entropy values range from 0 (complete uniformity, e.g., global synchrony) to 1 (maximal diversity of spatial patterns). This normalization allows for comparison across different system sizes and symbol sets. High entropy indicates a rich variety of spatial organizations, while low entropy reflects uniformity or strong clustering. This metric enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\n**Results: Synthetic Benchmarks**\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach distinguishes phase-locked states with identical levels of global synchronization but distinct spatial organization, as well as characterizes partially synchronized states. For example, in synthetic oscillator networks (\\(N = 100\\), ring topology, nearest-neighbor coupling), we show that spatial permutation entropy quantitatively differentiates between traveling waves (normalized \\(H \\approx 0.75\\)), chimera states (\\(H \\approx 0.5\\)), and globally synchronized regimes (\\(H \\approx 0.1\\)), even when traditional order parameters are similar. Figure 1A shows representative spatial ordinal pattern maps for each regime, illustrating the distinct spatial organizations captured by our method. Figure 1B plots the time course of normalized entropy during transitions between regimes, highlighting the sensitivity of our metric to transient dynamics.\n\nTo assess classification accuracy, we simulated 100 realizations of each regime and used a linear discriminant analysis (LDA) classifier trained on spatial permutation entropy and traditional metrics (global order parameter, amplitude variance). Our approach achieved up to 15% higher accuracy (mean improvement 12%, 95% CI [8%, 16%], Cohen's d = 1.1, \\(p < 0.01\\), permutation test, Bonferroni-corrected for three pairwise regime comparisons) in distinguishing regimes compared to traditional metrics alone (see Supplementary Table 1"}}